[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: Requested finite negative scalar resistance

[TEXT]
STATEMENT: >>>
Bounded original target: tests/xyce/Netlists/RESISTOR/resistor_neg.cir in pinned
RSpice 29c28a022625fd7cc2c56ead1cce2d3066663074. This is an unchanged Xyce
regression fixture retained by RSpice, outside the historical 113-deck ngspice
subtree. Its six DC points and original current observations must agree with
stock ngspice. The authored four-analysis example is separate and uncounted.

Reuse the existing typed self-controlled VCCS and its native DC/AC/transient
stamps. I(p to n) = (V(p)-V(n))/R is exact for a constant nonzero R of either
sign. A negative scalar is an active conductance; it does not relax the native
passive Resistor contract. No solver, dependency, schema or capacity change is
needed. This does not establish convergence or stability of every active circuit.
<<<

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: REQ-REQUESTED-NEGATIVE-SCALAR-R-001
STATUS: Draft
TITLE: Preserve finite signed scalar resistance and original identity
STATEMENT: >>>
Requested analysis preparation shall lower a supported plain finite negative
scalar resistance, including a braced scalar parameter expression, to a self-controlled
VCCS with output and control terminals in the original p/n order and Siemens
equal to its signed reciprocal. It shall retain original element provenance.
It shall refuse zero resistance and a negative value whose reciprocal is
nonfinite before publishing any requested operation. Positive resistors shall retain their
passive typed representation. Negative model, geometry and AC-only variants
remain outside this slice; legacy selected OP admission shall remain explicit.
<<<
COMMENT: >>>
Implementation: OperatingPointBridge requested scalar preparation and existing
Vccs DTO/native stamps. Verification: NegativeScalarResistorTests, including
128 FsCheck cases with replay 1001,2026111403, zero/overflow/model negatives,
original resistor_neg.cir, and examples/negative-scalar-resistor.cir.
The reproduced failing property minimized to NonNegativeInt 0 (R=-1 ohm).
Evidence: evidence/negative-scalar-resistor.json.
